s?", "Firms should consider how they establish they are dealing with an agent, what consents are in place, what the agent is allowed to do, how customers get support if something goes wrong, and whether to accept payments instructed by an agent or from an agent-controlled wallet."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto-united-kingdom/", "/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/"], "citations": [{"title": "Our approach to Agentic AI", "url": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/innovation/ai-approach/agentic-ai", "publisher": "Financial Conduct Authority", "date": "24 Nov 2026"}, {"title": "UK finance watchdog warns AI agent builders they may need a licence", "url": "https://thenextweb.com/news/fca-agentic-ai-authorisation", "publisher": "The Next Web", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "FCA: AI agents must comply with regulatory requirements", "url": "https://www.financialplanningtoday.co.uk/news/fca-ai-agents-must-comply-with-regulatory-requirements", "publisher": "Financial Planning Today", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Agentic AI in Financial Services: What Does the FCA Expect from Firms?", "url": "https://herrington-carmichael.com/insights/commercial-law/agentic-ai-in-financial-services-what-does-the-fca-expect-from-firms/", "publisher": "Herrington Carmichael", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Inside the LLM Call: GenAI Observability with OpenTelemetry", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog (James Newton-King)", "date": "14 May 2026"}]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>What the FCA said</h2>
<p>The FCA's new page on agentic AI is addressed to two audiences<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. To regulated firms, it says responsibility stays with the firm and its senior managers whether an agent is built in house or bought, and that firms must show they understand the systems they use, the degree of autonomy those systems have, the risks they create and the controls needed to manage them "in real time and in stress situations". It also asks firms to think about customers who arrive with their own agents: how to tell you are dealing with an agent, what consents exist, what the agent may do, and whether to accept payments an agent instructs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>To technology providers and platforms, it says to look at what their agents actually do. Payment services, financial promotions, account information services, credit broking and financial advice can all require authorisation, and describing a business as a technology provider does not change that<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Coverage noted the warning arrived as consumer agents that shop, book and pay move towards UK launch<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, and that the FCA will use its powers if regulated services are offered without authorisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this is an architecture question</h2>
<p>Read the expectations again with an engineer's eye. "Understand the degree of autonomy" means you can state, per action, what the agent may do alone. "Controls in real time" means the controls run in the request path, not in a quarterly review. "Establish that you are dealing with an agent" and "what consents are in place" are identity and authorisation requirements. "Support if something goes wrong" means you can reconstruct what happened. None of that can be bolted on after launch.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Intent</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Agent proposes an action<em>pay, apply, switch, invest</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Classification</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Activity map<em>is this regulated, and under whose permission?</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Authority</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Agent identity<em>which agent, for whom</em></span><span class="hl">Mandate and consent<em>limits, scope, expiry</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Execution</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Regulated partner or own permission<em>payments, credit, advice</em></span><span>Human step<em>where the risk demands it</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Decision record and trace<em>inputs, tools, outcome</em></span><span>Support and redress<em>a path for the customer</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Every money-moving agent action passes the same layers: classify, authorise, execute through the right party, record.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Four design rules that follow</h2>
<p><strong>1. Map actions to activities before you build.</strong> List every action the agent can take and decide, with legal advice, whether each is information, a promotion, advice, a payment or credit. That map decides where you need your own permissions, where a regulated partner executes, and what the agent must never do. The FCA's point that agents "evolve in a way that makes regulation apply" means the map must be re-checked whenever capabilities are added<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>2. Make the mandate explicit.</strong> An agent acting for a customer needs a machine-checkable mandate: which accounts, which payees, what limits, until when. Store it, show it to the customer and enforce it at execution time, not in the prompt.</p>
<p><strong>3. Put regulated steps behind a gateway, not inside the model.</strong> The model can propose; a deterministic service checks the activity map and mandate, then calls the payment or credit provider. Prompt injection then has a much harder time turning into money movement. The same separation appears in <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defences</a>.</p>
<p><strong>4. Record everything an investigator would ask.</strong> Senior managers are accountable for models in their area, including third-party ones<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. That needs evidence: the request, the agent's reasoning trace, the tools called, the mandate checked and the outcome. OpenTelemetry's GenAI conventions make this a by-product of normal tracing rather than a separate logging project<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/fca-agentic-ai-architecture-money-moving-agents/agent-trace.webp" alt="Trace view of an AI agent run showing nested spans for model calls and tool executions with durations, on an observability dashboard" width="1200" height="804" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>An agent run traced span by span: the raw material for showing a regulator, or a customer, what the agent did. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, James Newton-King</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The other side: firms receiving agent instructions</h2>
<p>Banks, insurers and payment firms now face customers' agents arriving at their APIs and websites. The FCA asks firms to consider whether to accept payments instructed by an agent at all, and what liabilities follow<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Commentators read this as a prompt to treat agent traffic as a distinct channel with its own controls<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Detect</b><span>Recognise agent traffic instead of treating it as a human or a bot to block</span></li>
<li><b>Identify</b><span>Bind the agent to a customer and a provider you can contact</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Check consent</b><span>Confirm the customer authorised this agent for this kind of action</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Limit</b><span>Apply agent-specific limits and step-up for high-risk actions</span></li>
<li><b>Record</b><span>Keep the instruction, consent and outcome together</span></li>
<li><b>Support</b><span>Give the customer a clear route when the agent got it wrong</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Treat agents as a channel: identify, check consent, limit, record, support.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="callout"><p>Blocking all agents is a decision, not a strategy. Customers will use them anyway; the firms that define a safe agent channel will set the terms.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The CTO's take</h2>
<p>The FCA has not written new rules, and it does not need to. Existing permissions, the Consumer Duty and senior manager accountability already reach agents. What changes is the build: an activity map, explicit mandates, a deterministic gateway for regulated steps, and traces good enough to defend a decision. Teams that build those in now can launch and adapt quickly; teams that do not will find every new capability turns into a compliance question. For related work, see the <a href="/case-studies/uk-payments-firm-ai-cyber-risk-controls/">UK payments AI controls case study</a>, <a href="/insights/ai-agents-can-pay-agentic-commerce/">agentic commerce</a> and my <a href="/fractional-cto-united-kingdom/">UK page</a>.</p>
<p>If you are building or receiving AI agents in UK financial services, I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> and <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. You can <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
